.KEY x
.BRA {
.KET }
;
; The loop half of S:btwatch. Never returns; it is started with Run.
;
; One-second cadence since 2026-08-26: the hang of ISSUE-0062 lands inside a
; five-second gap, so five seconds of resolution names no line. Each pass
; writes a heartbeat and then drains the Bluetooth error log.
; The heartbeat is the point: it is the only way to tell a machine that
; hung from a machine that is alive but no longer serving input. If the
; last line is a heartbeat several minutes before the reboot, the machine
; died; if heartbeats continue across the moment the keyboard froze, it
; did not.
;
; The heartbeat goes to BOTH the card and DEBUG:, on purpose. The card is
; the channel we trust -- serial output on this board stops somewhere
; around the Wanderer takeover (2026-08-26, cause not yet known). Sending
; the tick to DEBUG: as well turns that unknown into a measurement: the
; last tick on the serial line is the moment serial died, and comparing it
; against the same tick in the card log dates it exactly.
;
; The error-log dump stays on the card only: it is large, and if serial is
; already gone it would be written into nothing.
;
; BTErrorLog flushes what it prints, so each pass holds only what is new.
; Each redirection opens, appends and closes, so the card has the data
; even if the next pass never happens.
;
Echo >>SYS:btwatch.log "==== btwatch start ===="
Echo >DEBUG: "[btwatch] start"
Lab loop
Wait 1
Echo >DEBUG: "[btwatch] tick"
Date >DEBUG:
Date >>SYS:btwatch.log
BTErrorLog >>SYS:btwatch.log
Skip loop BACK
